# 19-WLD / wild bets

**Goes here.** Unconventional, one-off, asymmetric. Giveaways engineered for UGC, PR
stunts, personal outreach to high-value customers, surprise one-offs, community
rituals, founder moments, unexpected collaborations, viral loop mechanics, guerrilla
tactics, and how all of these backfire.

## The 2 tests. Both must pass.

1. **It is NOT a repeatable channel with its own economics.** If you can run it every
   week and compute a CAC, it belongs in its real module.
2. **Its payoff is ASYMMETRIC.** Bounded cost, unbounded or unmeasurable upside,
   usually invisible to standard attribution.

This folder has 1 failure mode: becoming the junk drawer where anything unusual gets
dumped. The 2 tests stop that.

## Where the line falls

```
giveaway that is really a discount                -> 03-OFF
giveaway engineered to produce 400 UGC posts      -> 19-WLD
handwritten note in every order, costed per order -> 17-CXP
personal letter to your 50 highest-value buyers   -> 19-WLD
referral program with tracked codes and a payback -> 03-OFF
referral stunt built on a one-off moment          -> 19-WLD
unconventional offer you could run every month    -> 03-OFF
```

The test is repeatability and measurability, not how unusual it feels. An
"unconventional offer strategy" that can be run repeatedly with a CAC is an offer.

**Roundup sources.** Documents that list 20 unconventional plays at once will contain
some that pass and some that do not. The pipeline should card each play separately and
route the repeatable ones to OFF or CXP.

## Header

```
---
url:                   # REQUIRED. Never blank.
rights: public_reference
why:
modules: [WLD]         # add OFF or CXP where a play is really repeatable
topics: []
tier: 4
cost_ceiling:          # REQUIRED. The bounded downside, in money and time.
scope_hint: growth
---
```

`cost_ceiling` is the point of the category. A wild bet with an unbounded downside is
not a wild bet, it is a gamble.

## Topics

giveaways and contests, PR stunts and activations, personalised outreach to
high-value customers, surprise and delight one-offs, community rituals and events,
founder moments, collaborations and unexpected partnerships, viral loop mechanics,
guerrilla and physical world tactics, how wild bets backfire.

## In the why line, capture 4 things if the source gives them

The mechanism, as a hypothesis not a finding. The cost ceiling. What would make it
repeatable if it works. How it backfires, because reputational risk is real and most
sources ignore it.

## Never invent an expected effect

Wild bets do not have reliable expected effects. Pretending otherwise poisons the rest
of the library.

## Retrieval rules

Hypothesis tier by default. May never outrank a baseline standard or a tested result.
Never recommended to a brand with an open baseline blocker. This folder is where the
exploration reserve draws from.

## How to collect

Opportunistically, never as a phase. Tier 4 is correct for most of it. A tier 4 bet
that gets tested once and works becomes your own tier 0 evidence, and nobody else will
have it.

